The DUKE’s chart room in the big house above the town. A table covered in maps, model ships and a decanter. The DUKE moves a model ship across a map. The DUCHESS reads a newspaper. The CONSTABLE stands at the door.

DUKE
If the light goes, the channel goes. If the channel goes, the harbour silts. If the harbour silts, I own a very large field of mud.

DUCHESS
You already own a very large field of mud. You call it the garden.

DUKE
Constable, what is this inspector like?

CONSTABLE
Thin, your grace. Wet. Asks questions.

DUKE
What kind of questions?

CONSTABLE
How many ships. How many wrecks. Whether the biscuits were for eating.

DUCHESS
(not looking up)
A sensible man.

DUKE
I shall invite him to dinner and explain the importance of the light with charts.

DUCHESS
Invite him to dinner and let *me* explain it. Without charts.

DUKE
My dear, you don’t know the first thing about navigation.

DUCHESS
I know the first thing about men from the Admiralty, which is that they are tired of being shown charts.
